### Ran Playwright code
```js
await page.evaluate('() => ({info: document.getElementById(\'info\')?.textContent, ready: !!window.__preview, err: window.__err || null})');
```

The model loads and looks like a proper M4/LVOA with stock, grip, magazine, rail, optic. But it's very dark (materials dark) and huge dimensions (15504 units long, x axis). Orientation: the rifle barrel points left (-x) in this view? Camera at (1.35,0.8,1.7) looking at origin; the model's long axis is X (15504), and barrel points toward -X (left) in screen. Good to know: forward = -X? Let's determine: gun stock at right, barrel at left. Camera is at +X+Z; screen-left corresponds roughly -X. So barrel likely points -X. model long axis X.

We need to rotate so barrel points -Z (three.js forward), rotate Y by -90°? Rotating -90 about Y maps -X to +Z or -Z? Rotation about Y by θ: x' = x cosθ + z sinθ; z' = -x sinθ + z cosθ. For a vector -X = (-1,0,0): θ=+90° → x'=0, z'=+1 (toward +Z, backward). θ=-90° → x'=0, z'=-1 (forward). So rotate Y by -90°.

Then scale: max dimension 15504 → target ~0.9 units. scale = 0.9/15504 = 5.8e-5. The gun would be 0.9 long, height 0.34, width 0.08. Good.

We need hands. The GLB has no hands. Our procedural hands could be attached, but scale mismatch; hands designed for our model dimensions (~0.9 long) so okay. Position right hand at grip, left at handguard. But materials of GLB are dark and flat; we can brighten materials and add wear via material tweaks. Also add our accent strips and flashlight? Not necessary.

Need muzzle position. We can compute from bbox in local model space: barrel tip at min X? After centering, barrel tip local x≈-7752 (size 15504/2). We can add an Object3D at the tip.

Also need to verify visual fidelity: the model looks good, but dark. It has 16 materials with baseColor linear values. In screen it's very dark because baseColor 0.06 linear ≈ sRGB 0.27, under lights 3.2. We can brighten/tint:
- For each material, set color to a lighter value? baseColorFactor is linear? In GLTF, baseColorFactor is linear. Three converts to material.color as linear? Three sets material.color.fromArray(linear?) I think GLTFLoader sets `material.color.setRGB(...)` with linear values; then renderer converts? In three, material.color is in linear space when ColorManagement enabled? Actually Color values are converted to working color space (linear) on setHex with SRGB, but glTF loader uses `material.color.setRGB(r,g,b, LinearSRGBColorSpace)`. So 0.06 linear is dark gray.

We can multiply colors by e.g. 2.2 and add slight blue-gray tint, set metalness/roughness per authored values, envMapIntensity. Or assign materials to our palette based on index/roughness:
- Most dark parts: color 0x23282a, roughness 0.6, metalness 0.5
- Tan parts (idx14): color 0x6b5b46.
- Some mid parts (idx9/10): 0x3a4144.
But then we lose authored distinctions. Could iterate each material and multiply its base color by 2.5, clamp. That preserves variation. Also set envMapIntensity 0.8.

Also, is the model Y-up with barrel horizontal? Yes.

Let's integrate into a new function `createGLBRifle()` in main.js or weapons.js. Since load is async, need to await before start. We can make main.js top-level await? The module already may have async initialization? Let's inspect main.js structure around rifle creation and game start. Top-level await is supported in ES modules and served over HTTP. But main.js currently creates rifle synchronously and the game may start menu. We can use `GLTFLoader.loadAsync` top-level await before creating the weaponRig, but that delays entire app module evaluation; loading 900KB locally is fast. Top-level await is acceptable, but if it fails, nothing loads. We can try/catch and fallback to procedural.

Better: keep weaponRig creation synchronous with procedural rifle, then asynchronously load GLB and replace rifle in weaponRig. But references to `rifle` (for muzzle flash, etc.) are used throughout. We can make a wrapper group that contains either model; easier: load GLB at startup with top-level await and use it.

Top-level await in module is fine in modern browsers and supported by import maps. Let's do:

```
const gltfRifle = await loadGLBRifle().catch(()=>null);
const rifle = gltfRifle || createRifle();
```

But main.js has lots of top-level code and exports debug handle; top-level await pauses the whole module until loaded — good, menu won't show until ready. 900KB + parse maybe 100ms.

Need import GLTFLoader in main.js, and a function to build the rifle. Put in weapons.js `loadRifleGLB(url)` async, returns root group with userData muzzle/eject/muzzleFlash/flashLight. But weapons.js imports flashRig internal; we can use inside.

Design:
```
export async function createRifleGLB(url) {
  const gltf = await new GLTFLoader().loadAsync(url);
  const root = new THREE.Group();
  const model = gltf.scene;
  // normalize: center bbox
  const box = new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(model);
  const size = box.getSize(new V3); const center = box.getCenter(new V3);
  model.position.sub(center); // center at origin
  // rotate so long axis X, barrel -X -> forward -Z
  model.rotation.y = -Math.PI/2;
  // after rotation, model's local X maps... but rotation applies to model as child; bbox re-evaluate maybe
  const holder = new THREE.Group();
  holder.add(model);
  holder.scale.setScalar(0.98 / maxDim);
  // Actually scale root holder
  root.add(holder);
